Z||000000||fropuff||09-30-2000||11:48 PM||||Hey, I was just reading some old posts about lowering.  I want to lower mine about 1-1.5 inches.  I do not want to cut the springs, nor do I want to buy some new ones.  I want to buy some, but there is NO WAY I can afford them now.  My $10 a week allowance doesn't go very far.  I need to save for the Cutlass anyway.  I was flipping through my JC Whitney, and I found some things that are called: "2-Way Coil Spring Adjusters/Stabilizers".  They are used on the coil springs.  They can either pinch 2 coils together to lower, or spread two out to raise.  It says they can raise or lower up to 2.75", "quickly, easily".  They are $4.98 for a pair of two, and eight of them would be needed.  I was wondering if anybody has used these.  They look like they would work, but JC isn't on the top of my list.  Again, I don't really want to buy some new springs to lower it just 1-1.5 inches.  Why don't I just find four fat kids at school to sit on the corners???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Thanks for the help.||||reg||4
Z||000001||fropuff||10-01-2000||10:48 AM||||Has anyone heard of/used these?  Anything good/bad about them?  If you want to take a look at them, go to:  <A HREF="http://www.jcwhitney.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jcwhitney.com/</A>   Then, on the left hand column about half way down, there is something bolded that says: "More General Products".  Click on this and drag down to "Suspension and Steering".  Then on the next page, click on "springs".  On the next page, they're on the very top.  They are now $5.98 a pair.  Need 4 pairs.  Tell me what you think.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-01-2000||10:57 AM||||Those are really ment for coils like the front ones. the coils on the strut springs are usually too far apart for that.<P>I know a number of people who've used them over the years. Those things have a nasty habit of breaking. usually at the worst time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Xtreme Fiero's||10-01-2000||11:13 AM||||you would have a better ride if you cut the springs. those clamps suck and you have to modify them to fit the back struts, the'll break and go through the only body pannel that doesn't un-bolt  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||10-01-2000||11:58 AM||||I used some on a Camaro a few years ago. The trouble that I had with them was that they would not stay where you put them. They started out on opposite sides of the spring, but over time would slide around until they were beside each other causing the spring to be really bent funny.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||10-01-2000||12:36 PM||||Thanks for the help.  I didn't think they would work.  Especially for $5.00 a set.  I don't want to cut the springs, especially of the things I've heard from some people on here.  I might just have to live with it.  I was looking at the front, and the space is about 2.5 inches.  I could get some 16 inch wheels, but I don't want to do that either.  I could try a slightly larger tire, but I don't want to dink around with the speedo calibration.  I might just have to live with it.  I don't really want my Fiero to have a stock appearance, but I'm all out of cash.  The Cutlass is in the shop this week getting a tune-up.  All that crap.  It also has a steering problem.  Talk about sloppy steering!  When I was going about 70 to the shop, the wind almost pushed me in the ditch.  I also had to over the train tracks at 55 so I didn't run out of gas going up a hill.  That pushed me in the right lane with a truck coming.  I think I need an anti-sway bar, springs, and shocks.  It also has an acceleration problem.  It took about 25 seconds to get to 60 on the highway.  That was all the way to the floor.  I let off a little, and it all of a sudden got a surge.  I'll see what's wrong with it.  Anyway, thanks for the help.  I just saw them, and I was thinking of if they would work.  Any way to save a little cash!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
